misc: hy310-motor: stop driving the limit switch and read it the way stock does

Homing on this board never finds the limit switch: PH14 reads LOW on every
poll, the run walks its full 100 UP + 250 DN mstep budget and ends in
"could not find limit switch!" (re/notes/WORKLOG.md, session 2026-03-21).
The conclusion drawn at the time -- a broken switch on the test unit --
does not survive the project's own stock captures.

Stock reads the same pin on the same board and gets a signal:

  * motor_control_probe (stock vmlinux 0xc05d753c) runs the homing block
    synchronously.  In the stock boot log the motor driver's own message is
    at 0.475418 s and the next kernel message at 0.483916 s
    (re/captures/HY310-DEV/boot.log:284).  8.5 ms is far too short for even
    one of the 100 mstep iterations (2 cycles x 8 phases x 1 ms each), so
    the loop provably did not run, so motor_limiter_status() returned 1 on
    the first read.  PH14 was HIGH.
  * limiter_num is 1, not 0: the stock GPIO dump lists
    "gpio-238 (limiter-up-gpio) in lo" and the log carries exactly one
    "failed to get property limiter-gpio" -- the one for the absent
    limiter-dn-gpio (re/captures/HY310-DEV/stock_gpio_debug.txt).

Three differences between that driver and this one, all in the read path:

1. The line was requested as an output.  devm_gpio_request_one() with flags
   == 0 is GPIOF_OUT_INIT_LOW, so the switch input is driven low by the
   SoC's output driver before gpiod_direction_input() corrects it, and that
   return value was discarded.  Stock claims the line with a bare
   gpio_request() and never gives it an output direction
   (motor_control_fdt_parse 0xc05d62c8, call sites 0xc05d65b4..0xc05d6620).
   Request it as GPIOF_IN, check the direction call, and fail the probe
   rather than home a motor whose limit input could not be made readable.

2. The pad bias was left to whatever the boot chain happened to leave.  On
   the running stock system PH14 is "input bias pull down"
   (re/captures/HY310-DEV/stock_pinconf_pio.txt), the defined idle level
   for active_level = 1.  Set the bias explicitly to the complement of
   active_level instead of inheriting an unspecified pad state.

3. The meaning of the limiter was inverted.  Stock's motor_run_up_control
   (0xc05d6ea0) backs off when the limiter reads INACTIVE, not active: the
   limit input is a range gate, active over the whole allowed travel, and
   leaving it is what makes stock reverse -- up to 40 msteps back until the
   limiter is active again, then back_step further steps, then latch the
   edge.  This driver backed off on the opposite condition, so with a
   limiter that never reads active it never backed off at all and simply
   kept stepping.  That is the path that drives the mechanism into its
   mechanical stop.  Mirror stock's condition.

Two further stock behaviours that were lost in the port and are restored
here, because both silently break movement:

  * Timing.  delay_ms_func (0xc05d6b00) is
    "autofocus ? n x const_udelay(0x1ffffc70) : usleep_range(800*n,1000*n)".
    Only the autofocus path busy-waits; a manual move sleeps.  This driver
    used mdelay() unconditionally and burned a CPU for the whole 5.6 s
    homing run.
  * Work handshake.  func_motor_run (0xc05d7a3c) keeps polling the queue for
    120 x 5 ms after it runs dry and only then clears work_is_running.  That
    grace period is what makes the "enqueue, then start the work only if it
    is not already running" handshake safe.  Draining the queue once and
    returning, as this driver did, loses every command enqueued between the
    last dequeue and clearing the flag -- which matches the parked report
    that sysfs moves stop working after boot.  motor_run_up_dn also has to
    start the work even when it rate-limits the enqueue away, as stock does.
    Beyond stock, the handler re-checks the queue after clearing the flag and
    restarts itself if something is waiting; stock leaves that last narrow
    window open, and remove() now empties the queue before cancelling so the
    re-arm cannot fight teardown.

The 100/250 mstep budget in homing stays.  It is not a workaround added
here: those counts are stock's own (0xc05d7758..0xc05d77c8).  They are a
bound, not the mechanism -- and when the limiter never reports in range the
driver now stops the phases, leaves both edge flags clear and says so,
instead of reporting an edge it did not observe.

The header comment is rewritten: stock has no interrupt path for the
limiter at all, so "H713 PH11+ EINT is broken" was never the reason for
polling, and the driver's own name is corrected in the description -- the
stock firmware has exactly one motor node and one motor driver, and it is
the focus drive.

Not verified: which of the three read-path defects the hardware will
actually clear.  The measurement that produced "PH14 always LOW" is from
2026-03-21, before patch 0030 fixed the malformed fan_power_hog, so at that
time the PB5 rail -- the board's shared backlight/fan power enable, which
stock holds high -- was provably off in our port.  Whether the limit sensor
hangs on that rail is unknown; TESTPLAN.md checks it first, before anything
moves.

